# Introduction to Cybersecurity — Cisco Networking Academy

## 🚀 About This Course

This was one of the first formal courses I took when I started learning cybersecurity seriously. It's Cisco's beginner-level introduction — covering the foundational concepts before getting into anything hands-on or technical. I went through it to build a real base before jumping into SOC labs on HTB and TryHackMe, rather than starting hands-on work without understanding the fundamentals first.

**Status:** ✅ Completed — certificate issued

---

## 📖 What It Covered

- The CIA Triad (Confidentiality, Integrity, Availability) and why it's the foundation most security decisions are built on
- Common types of threats and attacks — malware, social engineering, phishing, DoS/DDoS
- Basic cryptography concepts — how encryption protects data at rest and in transit
- Fundamentals of network security and defense-in-depth thinking
- The cybersecurity job landscape — different roles, career paths, and what each actually involves day to day

---

## 🧠 What I Actually Took From It

Going in, I already had a rough idea of what cybersecurity involved from reading around online, but this course was the first time I had the terminology properly organized instead of scattered. The CIA Triad specifically stuck with me — it's a simple framework, but I still catch myself using it when I'm trying to figure out why something is or isn't actually a security risk in later, more advanced material.

It's an entry-level course, so there's nothing here that's technically difficult if you've already done any hands-on labs. Its value for me was more about vocabulary and structure — having the right words for things I'd later see in practice on HTB and LetsDefend.

